      "worked_solution": "In general, the graph of $y=(x-h)^{2}$ looks like the standard parabola $y={x}^{2}$ but translated $h$ units right. The vertex of $y=(x-h)^{2}$ is located at $(h,0).$\n\nHere, the curve shown is the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ after being translated $8$ units *right*. The vertex is located at $(8,0).$\n\nTherefore, $h=8,$ and the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x-8)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
